    ($SATQ$dd428136$SATQ$, $SATQ$d8b61b96-0590-43d3-95e7-394b70d0c761$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$3056b358-628e-4a83-bf21-d6af15caa244$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$FSS$SATQ$, $SATQ$Form, Structure, and Sense$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Cheng Dang and her colleagues at the University of Washington recently ran simulations to determine the extent to which individual snow <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> affect the amount of light reflecting off a snowy surface.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"e0e887ab-b228-4715-ab73-46ffb2346511","content":"<p>grain&rsquo;s physical properties&rsquo;</p>"},{"id":"6e351da8-0756-4805-972e-14c30b41e99a","content":"<p>grains&rsquo; physical properties</p>"},{"id":"5a316c46-9916-4b54-90f6-58e466ae4834","content":"<p>grains&rsquo; physical property&rsquo;s</p>"},{"id":"cd41484d-c1c4-4667-99e4-6965eabaf5ec","content":"<p>grains physical properties</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["6e351da8-0756-4805-972e-14c30b41e99a"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of plural and possessive nouns. The plural possessive noun &ldquo;grains&rsquo;&rdquo; and the plural noun &ldquo;properties&rdquo; correctly indicate that the simulations involved multiple snow grains and that those snow grains had several properties. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the context requires the plural possessive noun &ldquo;grains&rsquo;&rdquo; and the plural noun &ldquo;properties,&rdquo; not the singular possessive noun &ldquo;grain&rsquo;s&rdquo; and the plural possessive noun &ldquo;properties&rsquo;.&rdquo; Choice C is incorrect because the context requires the plural noun &ldquo;properties,&rdquo; not the singular possessive noun &ldquo;property&rsquo;s.&rdquo; Choice D is incorrect because the context requires the plural possessive noun &ldquo;grains&rsquo;,&rdquo; not the plural noun &ldquo;grains.&rdquo; </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-675-SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$514496a9-a6fe-4b1f-a3a5-8d533ce00f83$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"dd428136","external_id":"d8b61b96-0590-43d3-95e7-394b70d0c761","vaultid":"3056b358-628e-4a83-bf21-d6af15caa244","uId":"75650e68-abbf-4ed5-8665-8128ad73e782","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"FSS","skill_desc":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Cheng Dang and her colleagues at the University of Washington recently ran simulations to determine the extent to which individual snow <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> affect the amount of light reflecting off a snowy surface.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"e0e887ab-b228-4715-ab73-46ffb2346511","content":"<p>grain&rsquo;s physical properties&rsquo;</p>"},{"id":"6e351da8-0756-4805-972e-14c30b41e99a","content":"<p>grains&rsquo; physical properties</p>"},{"id":"5a316c46-9916-4b54-90f6-58e466ae4834","content":"<p>grains&rsquo; physical property&rsquo;s</p>"},{"id":"cd41484d-c1c4-4667-99e4-6965eabaf5ec","content":"<p>grains physical properties</p>"}],"keys":["6e351da8-0756-4805-972e-14c30b41e99a"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of plural and possessive nouns. The plural possessive noun &ldquo;grains&rsquo;&rdquo; and the plural noun &ldquo;properties&rdquo; correctly indicate that the simulations involved multiple snow grains and that those snow grains had several properties. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the context requires the plural possessive noun &ldquo;grains&rsquo;&rdquo; and the plural noun &ldquo;properties,&rdquo; not the singular possessive noun &ldquo;grain&rsquo;s&rdquo; and the plural possessive noun &ldquo;properties&rsquo;.&rdquo; Choice C is incorrect because the context requires the plural noun &ldquo;properties,&rdquo; not the singular possessive noun &ldquo;property&rsquo;s.&rdquo; Choice D is incorrect because the context requires the plural possessive noun &ldquo;grains&rsquo;,&rdquo; not the plural noun &ldquo;grains.&rdquo; </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-675-SEC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"514496a9-a6fe-4b1f-a3a5-8d533ce00f83","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959346,"updateDate":1691007959346}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959346, 1691007959346),
    ($SATQ$dd6a0326$SATQ$, $SATQ$75e2f271-deb9-48a5-8790-d06ec8eb3379$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$df52eb59-63f0-4704-ab81-499eea8bbc5d$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$FSS$SATQ$, $SATQ$Form, Structure, and Sense$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>African American Percy Julian was a scientist and entrepreneur whose work helped people around the world to see. Named in 1999 as one of the greatest achievements by a US chemist in the past hundred years, <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> led to the first mass-produced treatment for glaucoma.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"d31a3c83-001f-47f5-a97a-830ba18dee07","content":"<p>Julian synthesized the alkaloid physostigmine in 1935; it&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"91293f81-7387-43e6-9d47-401966458b5f","content":"<p>in 1935 Julian synthesized the alkaloid physostigmine, which</p>"},{"id":"f62b0f21-b627-47e2-92d3-0a92725e3fc5","content":"<p>Julian&rsquo;s 1935 synthesis of the alkaloid physostigmine</p>"},{"id":"a4a735a0-cb61-4182-9d95-52aef10906da","content":"<p>the alkaloid physostigmine was synthesized by Julian in 1935 and</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["f62b0f21-b627-47e2-92d3-0a92725e3fc5"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. The convention being tested is subject-modifier placement. This choice makes the noun phrase &ldquo;Julian&rsquo;s 1935 synthesis&rdquo; the subject of the sentence and places it immediately after the modifying phrase &ldquo;named&hellip;years.&rdquo; In doing so, this choice clearly establishes that Julian&rsquo;s 1935 synthesis of the alkaloid physostigmine&mdash;and not another noun in the sentence&mdash;was named in 1999 as one of the greatest achievements by a US chemist in the past hundred years. &nbsp;</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it results in a dangling modifier. The placement of the noun &ldquo;Julian&rdquo; immediately after the modifying phrase illogically suggests that Julian himself was named as one of the greatest achievements by a US chemist in the past hundred years. Choice B is incorrect because it results in a dangling modifier. The placement of the prepositional phrase &ldquo;in 1935&rdquo; immediately after the modifying phrase illogically and confusingly suggests that &ldquo;in 1935&rdquo; was named as one of the greatest achievements by a US chemist in the past hundred years. Choice D is incorrect because it results in a dangling modifier. The placement of the noun phrase &ldquo;the alkaloid physostigmine&rdquo; immediately after the modifying phrase illogically and confusingly suggests that the alkaloid physostigmine itself (not the synthesis of it) was named as one of the greatest achievements by a US chemist in the past hundred years. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-685-SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$56e8e920-fd8e-46b7-b252-6237fdc2b701$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"dd6a0326","external_id":"75e2f271-deb9-48a5-8790-d06ec8eb3379","vaultid":"df52eb59-63f0-4704-ab81-499eea8bbc5d","uId":"6e3093d9-30cc-43a7-aff1-dbbeec781027","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"FSS","skill_desc":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>African American Percy Julian was a scientist and entrepreneur whose work helped people around the world to see. Named in 1999 as one of the greatest achievements by a US chemist in the past hundred years, <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> led to the first mass-produced treatment for glaucoma.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"d31a3c83-001f-47f5-a97a-830ba18dee07","content":"<p>Julian synthesized the alkaloid physostigmine in 1935; it&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"91293f81-7387-43e6-9d47-401966458b5f","content":"<p>in 1935 Julian synthesized the alkaloid physostigmine, which</p>"},{"id":"f62b0f21-b627-47e2-92d3-0a92725e3fc5","content":"<p>Julian&rsquo;s 1935 synthesis of the alkaloid physostigmine</p>"},{"id":"a4a735a0-cb61-4182-9d95-52aef10906da","content":"<p>the alkaloid physostigmine was synthesized by Julian in 1935 and</p>"}],"keys":["f62b0f21-b627-47e2-92d3-0a92725e3fc5"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. The convention being tested is subject-modifier placement. This choice makes the noun phrase &ldquo;Julian&rsquo;s 1935 synthesis&rdquo; the subject of the sentence and places it immediately after the modifying phrase &ldquo;named&hellip;years.&rdquo; In doing so, this choice clearly establishes that Julian&rsquo;s 1935 synthesis of the alkaloid physostigmine&mdash;and not another noun in the sentence&mdash;was named in 1999 as one of the greatest achievements by a US chemist in the past hundred years. &nbsp;</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it results in a dangling modifier. The placement of the noun &ldquo;Julian&rdquo; immediately after the modifying phrase illogically suggests that Julian himself was named as one of the greatest achievements by a US chemist in the past hundred years. Choice B is incorrect because it results in a dangling modifier. The placement of the prepositional phrase &ldquo;in 1935&rdquo; immediately after the modifying phrase illogically and confusingly suggests that &ldquo;in 1935&rdquo; was named as one of the greatest achievements by a US chemist in the past hundred years. Choice D is incorrect because it results in a dangling modifier. The placement of the noun phrase &ldquo;the alkaloid physostigmine&rdquo; immediately after the modifying phrase illogically and confusingly suggests that the alkaloid physostigmine itself (not the synthesis of it) was named as one of the greatest achievements by a US chemist in the past hundred years. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-685-SEC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"56e8e920-fd8e-46b7-b252-6237fdc2b701","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959346,"updateDate":1691007959346}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959346, 1691007959346),
    ($SATQ$dddfa043$SATQ$, $SATQ$4ef8ef57-da46-45e4-8761-ac1b2742b33d$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$a9e3dd29-279c-4d88-8a3a-1cfd56e5504f$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$BOU$SATQ$, $SATQ$Boundaries$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Before the Erie Canal was completed in 1825, transporting goods by wagon between New York City and the Midwest took up to forty-five days and cost one hundred dollars per ton. By linking the Hudson River to Lake <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> canal reduced transport time to nine days and cut costs to six dollars per ton.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"1a7c44ff-b2e6-4e62-8394-8fc04f4de958","content":"<p>Erie; the</p>"},{"id":"e7d2c102-ed9e-4f49-bae4-84b19aaf8541","content":"<p>Erie (the</p>"},{"id":"4da1bd96-7c35-46b0-84f6-5e8ba2a014d5","content":"<p>Erie, the</p>"},{"id":"b24fd9c4-27a8-430c-8a84-024cd9bac504","content":"<p>Erie: the</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["4da1bd96-7c35-46b0-84f6-5e8ba2a014d5"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. The convention being tested is punctuation between a supplementary phrase and a main clause. This choice correctly uses a comma to mark the boundary between the introductory supplementary phrase (&ldquo;By linking the Hudson River to Lake Erie&rdquo;), which identifies how the canal reduced transport time, and the main clause (&ldquo;the canal reduced transport time to nine days and cut costs to six dollars per ton&rdquo;). </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because a semicolon can&rsquo;t be used in this way to mark the boundary between a supplementary phrase (&ldquo;By&hellip;Erie&rdquo;) and the main clause (&ldquo;the canal...ton&rdquo;). Choice B is incorrect because an open parenthesis can&rsquo;t be used in this way to mark the boundary between a supplementary phrase (&ldquo;By&hellip;Erie&rdquo;) and the main clause (&ldquo;the canal...ton&rdquo;). Choice D is incorrect because a colon can&rsquo;t be used in this way to mark the boundary between an introductory supplementary phrase (&ldquo;By&hellip;Erie&rdquo;) and the main clause (&ldquo;the canal...ton&rdquo;). </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-775-SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$fcfb1165-7ae2-4722-a819-d38c7c4cbf2e$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"dddfa043","external_id":"4ef8ef57-da46-45e4-8761-ac1b2742b33d","vaultid":"a9e3dd29-279c-4d88-8a3a-1cfd56e5504f","uId":"bf139604-f304-4333-ab35-06ca471f5bc6","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"BOU","skill_desc":"Boundaries","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":2,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Before the Erie Canal was completed in 1825, transporting goods by wagon between New York City and the Midwest took up to forty-five days and cost one hundred dollars per ton. By linking the Hudson River to Lake <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> canal reduced transport time to nine days and cut costs to six dollars per ton.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"1a7c44ff-b2e6-4e62-8394-8fc04f4de958","content":"<p>Erie; the</p>"},{"id":"e7d2c102-ed9e-4f49-bae4-84b19aaf8541","content":"<p>Erie (the</p>"},{"id":"4da1bd96-7c35-46b0-84f6-5e8ba2a014d5","content":"<p>Erie, the</p>"},{"id":"b24fd9c4-27a8-430c-8a84-024cd9bac504","content":"<p>Erie: the</p>"}],"keys":["4da1bd96-7c35-46b0-84f6-5e8ba2a014d5"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. The convention being tested is punctuation between a supplementary phrase and a main clause. This choice correctly uses a comma to mark the boundary between the introductory supplementary phrase (&ldquo;By linking the Hudson River to Lake Erie&rdquo;), which identifies how the canal reduced transport time, and the main clause (&ldquo;the canal reduced transport time to nine days and cut costs to six dollars per ton&rdquo;). </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because a semicolon can&rsquo;t be used in this way to mark the boundary between a supplementary phrase (&ldquo;By&hellip;Erie&rdquo;) and the main clause (&ldquo;the canal...ton&rdquo;). Choice B is incorrect because an open parenthesis can&rsquo;t be used in this way to mark the boundary between a supplementary phrase (&ldquo;By&hellip;Erie&rdquo;) and the main clause (&ldquo;the canal...ton&rdquo;). Choice D is incorrect because a colon can&rsquo;t be used in this way to mark the boundary between an introductory supplementary phrase (&ldquo;By&hellip;Erie&rdquo;) and the main clause (&ldquo;the canal...ton&rdquo;). </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-775-SEC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"fcfb1165-7ae2-4722-a819-d38c7c4cbf2e","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959352,"updateDate":1691007959352}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959352, 1691007959352),
    ($SATQ$de3dd17d$SATQ$, $SATQ$fb98819c-e3c7-4182-986d-6e3fe6ba6ec1$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$e360be23-f165-400b-a3da-0c4ee4df4345$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$FSS$SATQ$, $SATQ$Form, Structure, and Sense$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Planetary scientist Briony Horgan and her colleagues have determined that as much as 25 percent of the sand on Mars is composed of impact spherules. These spherical bits of glass form when asteroids collide with the planet, ejecting bits of molten rock into the atmosphere that, after cooling and solidifying into glass, <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> back onto Mars&rsquo;s surface.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"08ea8153-cde5-41c8-a510-fc39f7268049","content":"<p>to rain</p>"},{"id":"47d9d260-8ca6-45d8-85de-1945b7282033","content":"<p>raining</p>"},{"id":"be847f1b-06af-441a-9eaf-cce2664df307","content":"<p>having rained</p>"},{"id":"3e7b3bd0-13b6-4753-9799-a466ac638e87","content":"<p>rain</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["3e7b3bd0-13b6-4753-9799-a466ac638e87"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. "That&hellip;[rain] back onto Mars&rsquo;s surface" is a relative clause that describes the "bits of molten rock." Forming the clause requires a conjugated, finite verb, and this is the only choice that provides that.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. "To rain" is an infinitive and can&rsquo;t serve as the main verb of a clause. A conjugated verb is needed here to form the main verb of the relative clause "that&hellip;[rain] back onto Mars&rsquo;s surface," which describes the "bits of molten rock." Choice B is incorrect. "Raining" is a present participle and, on its own, can&rsquo;t serve as the main verb of a clause. A conjugated verb is needed here to form the main verb of the relative clause "that&hellip;[rain] back onto Mars&rsquo;s surface," which describes the "bits of molten rock." Choice C is incorrect. "Having rained" is a perfect participle and can&rsquo;t serve as the main verb of a clause. A conjugated verb is needed here to form the main verb of the relative clause "that&hellip;[rain] back onto Mars&rsquo;s surface," which describes the "bits of molten rock."</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-FSS-H010$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$e389232f-5549-4571-bbfa-b59222340695$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"de3dd17d","external_id":"fb98819c-e3c7-4182-986d-6e3fe6ba6ec1","vaultid":"e360be23-f165-400b-a3da-0c4ee4df4345","uId":"84596316-efcf-4300-ae02-52e992eb6008","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"FSS","skill_desc":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Planetary scientist Briony Horgan and her colleagues have determined that as much as 25 percent of the sand on Mars is composed of impact spherules. These spherical bits of glass form when asteroids collide with the planet, ejecting bits of molten rock into the atmosphere that, after cooling and solidifying into glass, <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> back onto Mars&rsquo;s surface.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"08ea8153-cde5-41c8-a510-fc39f7268049","content":"<p>to rain</p>"},{"id":"47d9d260-8ca6-45d8-85de-1945b7282033","content":"<p>raining</p>"},{"id":"be847f1b-06af-441a-9eaf-cce2664df307","content":"<p>having rained</p>"},{"id":"3e7b3bd0-13b6-4753-9799-a466ac638e87","content":"<p>rain</p>"}],"keys":["3e7b3bd0-13b6-4753-9799-a466ac638e87"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. \"That&hellip;[rain] back onto Mars&rsquo;s surface\" is a relative clause that describes the \"bits of molten rock.\" Forming the clause requires a conjugated, finite verb, and this is the only choice that provides that.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. \"To rain\" is an infinitive and can&rsquo;t serve as the main verb of a clause. A conjugated verb is needed here to form the main verb of the relative clause \"that&hellip;[rain] back onto Mars&rsquo;s surface,\" which describes the \"bits of molten rock.\" Choice B is incorrect. \"Raining\" is a present participle and, on its own, can&rsquo;t serve as the main verb of a clause. A conjugated verb is needed here to form the main verb of the relative clause \"that&hellip;[rain] back onto Mars&rsquo;s surface,\" which describes the \"bits of molten rock.\" Choice C is incorrect. \"Having rained\" is a perfect participle and can&rsquo;t serve as the main verb of a clause. A conjugated verb is needed here to form the main verb of the relative clause \"that&hellip;[rain] back onto Mars&rsquo;s surface,\" which describes the \"bits of molten rock.\"</p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-FSS-H010","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"e389232f-5549-4571-bbfa-b59222340695","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959367,"updateDate":1691007959367}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959367, 1691007959367),
    ($SATQ$de55ec71$SATQ$, $SATQ$523887d8-5850-4f95-828e-c64095efdb88$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$b5fe4895-4b12-4ccc-8e6d-569a453c9881$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$BOU$SATQ$, $SATQ$Boundaries$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Generations of mystery and horror <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> have been influenced by the dark, gothic stories of celebrated American author Edgar Allan Poe (1809&ndash;1849).</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"adbcc70c-1ad7-40d1-9ba6-978f30f5714f","content":"<p>writers</p>"},{"id":"3d67289f-09a1-4fb1-addd-6a569a441129","content":"<p>writers,</p>"},{"id":"fcc2c90d-7fcd-43b1-92ed-a9866e1f4b1b","content":"<p>writers&mdash;</p>"},{"id":"e16e00f5-02e3-498e-9010-7eef1b3a9d9f","content":"<p>writers;</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["adbcc70c-1ad7-40d1-9ba6-978f30f5714f"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. The convention being tested is punctuation between a subject and a verb. When, as in this case, a subject (&ldquo;Generations of mystery and horror writers&rdquo;) is immediately followed by a verb (&ldquo;have been influenced&rdquo;), no punctuation is needed. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between the subject and the verb. Choice C is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between the subject and the verb. Choice D is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between the subject and the verb. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-774-SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$3cbbb8db-0c3d-40f5-a2a8-14fcde26f834$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"de55ec71","external_id":"523887d8-5850-4f95-828e-c64095efdb88","vaultid":"b5fe4895-4b12-4ccc-8e6d-569a453c9881","uId":"009ed63c-79c6-4cde-91dd-b6f4d390288c","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"BOU","skill_desc":"Boundaries","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":2,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Generations of mystery and horror <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> have been influenced by the dark, gothic stories of celebrated American author Edgar Allan Poe (1809&ndash;1849).</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"adbcc70c-1ad7-40d1-9ba6-978f30f5714f","content":"<p>writers</p>"},{"id":"3d67289f-09a1-4fb1-addd-6a569a441129","content":"<p>writers,</p>"},{"id":"fcc2c90d-7fcd-43b1-92ed-a9866e1f4b1b","content":"<p>writers&mdash;</p>"},{"id":"e16e00f5-02e3-498e-9010-7eef1b3a9d9f","content":"<p>writers;</p>"}],"keys":["adbcc70c-1ad7-40d1-9ba6-978f30f5714f"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer. The convention being tested is punctuation between a subject and a verb. When, as in this case, a subject (&ldquo;Generations of mystery and horror writers&rdquo;) is immediately followed by a verb (&ldquo;have been influenced&rdquo;), no punctuation is needed. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between the subject and the verb. Choice C is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between the subject and the verb. Choice D is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between the subject and the verb. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-774-SEC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"3cbbb8db-0c3d-40f5-a2a8-14fcde26f834","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959352,"updateDate":1691007959352}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959352, 1691007959352),
    ($SATQ$dfbf5d33$SATQ$, $SATQ$6acf38a5-775d-4334-8111-a9f1394d9db3$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$675e4a5f-f684-4813-a13e-da616864fff1$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$FSS$SATQ$, $SATQ$Form, Structure, and Sense$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>In 1453, English King Henry VI became unfit to rule after falling gravely ill. As a result, Parliament appointed Richard, Third Duke of York, who had a strong claim to the English throne, to rule as Lord Protector. Upon recovering two years later, <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> forcing an angered Richard from the royal court and precipitating a series of battles later known as the Wars of the Roses.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"449dc3aa-2a7e-4d62-8454-d4b6c9b7ecfd","content":"<p>Henry resumed his reign,</p>"},{"id":"51104bdd-3ab1-4f93-8d71-03b7bab5acaf","content":"<p>the reign of Henry resumed,</p>"},{"id":"39d8c1b8-73d4-4657-ba38-10921e1890a3","content":"<p>Henry&rsquo;s reign resumed,</p>"},{"id":"85191351-a62d-43ca-a1ae-ab669314ae6e","content":"<p>it was Henry who resumed his reign,&nbsp;</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["449dc3aa-2a7e-4d62-8454-d4b6c9b7ecfd"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. The convention being tested is subject-modifier placement. This choice ensures that the introductory phrase &ldquo;upon recovering two years later&rdquo; appears immediately before the noun it modifies (&ldquo;Henry&rdquo;), clearly establishing that Henry recovered two years later. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect because it results in a dangling modifier. The placement of the noun phrase &ldquo;the reign of Henry&rdquo; immediately after the introductory phrase illogically suggests that the reign of Henry recovered two years later.&nbsp;Choice C is incorrect because it results in a dangling modifier. The placement of the noun phrase &ldquo;Henry&rsquo;s reign&rdquo; immediately after the introductory phrase illogically suggests that Henry&rsquo;s reign recovered two years later.&nbsp;Choice D is incorrect because it results in a dangling modifier. The placement of the function word &ldquo;it&rdquo; immediately after the introductory phrase illogically suggests that &ldquo;it&rdquo; recovered two years later. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-691-SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$eb5da575-84ee-4aa5-b81a-51c54126dcba$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"dfbf5d33","external_id":"6acf38a5-775d-4334-8111-a9f1394d9db3","vaultid":"675e4a5f-f684-4813-a13e-da616864fff1","uId":"e0552499-380f-4851-91fe-26102a97a228","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"FSS","skill_desc":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>In 1453, English King Henry VI became unfit to rule after falling gravely ill. As a result, Parliament appointed Richard, Third Duke of York, who had a strong claim to the English throne, to rule as Lord Protector. Upon recovering two years later, <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> forcing an angered Richard from the royal court and precipitating a series of battles later known as the Wars of the Roses.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"449dc3aa-2a7e-4d62-8454-d4b6c9b7ecfd","content":"<p>Henry resumed his reign,</p>"},{"id":"51104bdd-3ab1-4f93-8d71-03b7bab5acaf","content":"<p>the reign of Henry resumed,</p>"},{"id":"39d8c1b8-73d4-4657-ba38-10921e1890a3","content":"<p>Henry&rsquo;s reign resumed,</p>"},{"id":"85191351-a62d-43ca-a1ae-ab669314ae6e","content":"<p>it was Henry who resumed his reign,&nbsp;</p>"}],"keys":["449dc3aa-2a7e-4d62-8454-d4b6c9b7ecfd"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer. The convention being tested is subject-modifier placement. This choice ensures that the introductory phrase &ldquo;upon recovering two years later&rdquo; appears immediately before the noun it modifies (&ldquo;Henry&rdquo;), clearly establishing that Henry recovered two years later. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect because it results in a dangling modifier. The placement of the noun phrase &ldquo;the reign of Henry&rdquo; immediately after the introductory phrase illogically suggests that the reign of Henry recovered two years later.&nbsp;Choice C is incorrect because it results in a dangling modifier. The placement of the noun phrase &ldquo;Henry&rsquo;s reign&rdquo; immediately after the introductory phrase illogically suggests that Henry&rsquo;s reign recovered two years later.&nbsp;Choice D is incorrect because it results in a dangling modifier. The placement of the function word &ldquo;it&rdquo; immediately after the introductory phrase illogically suggests that &ldquo;it&rdquo; recovered two years later. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-691-SEC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"eb5da575-84ee-4aa5-b81a-51c54126dcba","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959346,"updateDate":1691007959346}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959346, 1691007959346),
    ($SATQ$e060dd6b$SATQ$, $SATQ$bc1744e8-0f12-494e-9ed7-58cc89eacd1c$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$FSS$SATQ$, $SATQ$Form, Structure, and Sense$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Recordings of electrical activity in the brain, <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> increased activity in brain areas associated with suppressing motor functions.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"a0ce6c56-2707-4881-b498-439a75f48c7f","content":"<p>electrograms show that while responding to hypothetical match scenarios, the most highly skilled soccer players have</p>"},{"id":"0eb9dce5-89f3-47d1-8972-c5c4b3c17daa","content":"<p>the most highly skilled soccer players responding to hypothetical match scenarios have electrograms that show</p>"},{"id":"351d9e5a-8220-4375-90b4-5ba02fb32fad","content":"<p>responses to hypothetical match scenarios show that the most highly skilled soccer players have electrograms with</p>"},{"id":"9f367917-0896-460f-b9d8-c65a190cba58","content":"<p>hypothetical match scenario responses show that the most highly skilled soccer players captured in electrograms have</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["a0ce6c56-2707-4881-b498-439a75f48c7f"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. The convention being tested is subject-modifier placement. This choice makes the noun &ldquo;electrograms&rdquo; the subject of the sentence and places it immediately after the modifying phrase &ldquo;recordings of electrical activity in the brain.&rdquo; In doing so, this choice clearly establishes that electrograms&mdash;and not other nouns or noun phrases in the sentence&mdash;are recordings of electrical activity in the brain.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because it results in a dangling modifier. The placement of the noun phrase &ldquo;the most highly skilled soccer players&rdquo; immediately after the modifying phrase illogically suggests that the players are recordings of electrical activity in the brain. Choice C is incorrect because it results in a dangling modifier. The placement of the noun phrase &ldquo;responses to hypothetical match scenarios&rdquo; immediately after the modifying phrase illogically suggests that these responses are recordings of electrical activity in the brain. Choice D is incorrect because it results in a dangling modifier. The placement of the noun phrase &ldquo;hypothetical match scenario responses&rdquo; immediately after the opening phrase illogically suggests that these responses are recordings of electrical activity in the brain.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"e060dd6b","external_id":"bc1744e8-0f12-494e-9ed7-58cc89eacd1c","vaultid":null,"uId":"31a9adcf-6b6c-434d-ab5d-e6616a220f58","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"FSS","skill_desc":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Recordings of electrical activity in the brain, <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> increased activity in brain areas associated with suppressing motor functions.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"a0ce6c56-2707-4881-b498-439a75f48c7f","content":"<p>electrograms show that while responding to hypothetical match scenarios, the most highly skilled soccer players have</p>"},{"id":"0eb9dce5-89f3-47d1-8972-c5c4b3c17daa","content":"<p>the most highly skilled soccer players responding to hypothetical match scenarios have electrograms that show</p>"},{"id":"351d9e5a-8220-4375-90b4-5ba02fb32fad","content":"<p>responses to hypothetical match scenarios show that the most highly skilled soccer players have electrograms with</p>"},{"id":"9f367917-0896-460f-b9d8-c65a190cba58","content":"<p>hypothetical match scenario responses show that the most highly skilled soccer players captured in electrograms have</p>"}],"keys":["a0ce6c56-2707-4881-b498-439a75f48c7f"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer. The convention being tested is subject-modifier placement. This choice makes the noun &ldquo;electrograms&rdquo; the subject of the sentence and places it immediately after the modifying phrase &ldquo;recordings of electrical activity in the brain.&rdquo; In doing so, this choice clearly establishes that electrograms&mdash;and not other nouns or noun phrases in the sentence&mdash;are recordings of electrical activity in the brain.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because it results in a dangling modifier. The placement of the noun phrase &ldquo;the most highly skilled soccer players&rdquo; immediately after the modifying phrase illogically suggests that the players are recordings of electrical activity in the brain. Choice C is incorrect because it results in a dangling modifier. The placement of the noun phrase &ldquo;responses to hypothetical match scenarios&rdquo; immediately after the modifying phrase illogically suggests that these responses are recordings of electrical activity in the brain. Choice D is incorrect because it results in a dangling modifier. The placement of the noun phrase &ldquo;hypothetical match scenario responses&rdquo; immediately after the opening phrase illogically suggests that these responses are recordings of electrical activity in the brain.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1776362771170,"updateDate":1776362771170}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1776362771170, 1776362771170),
    ($SATQ$e15c50b2$SATQ$, $SATQ$dcb7a16b-f736-4733-af9b-04a8b4f35eb2$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$5f746365-df99-4e4a-84b9-b6f506e5c501$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$BOU$SATQ$, $SATQ$Boundaries$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>A standard Argo float, a type of autonomous robot, measures temperature and salinity in the upper regions of ice-free oceans. More advanced floats can measure a wider range of <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> and monitor seasonal ice zones. </p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"078b13c5-36b2-4291-a908-f8dfe960cabf","content":"<p>variables: travel to greater depths</p>"},{"id":"55e37fdc-a384-479a-a47f-ad332ac217bb","content":"<p>variables, travel to greater depths,</p>"},{"id":"e870cd3d-a9b6-4819-b72c-eead6b265ccf","content":"<p>variables travel to greater depths,</p>"},{"id":"c25cb845-f540-4a52-8c20-b4494651e3c2","content":"<p>variables, travel to greater depths;</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["55e37fdc-a384-479a-a47f-ad332ac217bb"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer. The convention being tested is the punctuation of items in a series. The comma after &ldquo;variables&rdquo; is used conventionally to separate the first and second items in the series, and the comma after &ldquo;depths&rdquo; is used conventionally to separate the second and third items. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because a colon can&rsquo;t be used in this way to separate items in a simple series. Choice C is incorrect because a comma is needed after &ldquo;variables&rdquo; to separate the first and second items in the series. Choice D is incorrect because a semicolon can&rsquo;t be used in this way to separate items in a simple series. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-663-SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$e8513e3e-e344-4549-a27c-848c952e8acc$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"e15c50b2","external_id":"dcb7a16b-f736-4733-af9b-04a8b4f35eb2","vaultid":"5f746365-df99-4e4a-84b9-b6f506e5c501","uId":"ad0c3988-b4d4-4f8a-8b86-f3f17789b860","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"BOU","skill_desc":"Boundaries","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>A standard Argo float, a type of autonomous robot, measures temperature and salinity in the upper regions of ice-free oceans. More advanced floats can measure a wider range of <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> and monitor seasonal ice zones. </p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"078b13c5-36b2-4291-a908-f8dfe960cabf","content":"<p>variables: travel to greater depths</p>"},{"id":"55e37fdc-a384-479a-a47f-ad332ac217bb","content":"<p>variables, travel to greater depths,</p>"},{"id":"e870cd3d-a9b6-4819-b72c-eead6b265ccf","content":"<p>variables travel to greater depths,</p>"},{"id":"c25cb845-f540-4a52-8c20-b4494651e3c2","content":"<p>variables, travel to greater depths;</p>"}],"keys":["55e37fdc-a384-479a-a47f-ad332ac217bb"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer. The convention being tested is the punctuation of items in a series. The comma after &ldquo;variables&rdquo; is used conventionally to separate the first and second items in the series, and the comma after &ldquo;depths&rdquo; is used conventionally to separate the second and third items. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because a colon can&rsquo;t be used in this way to separate items in a simple series. Choice C is incorrect because a comma is needed after &ldquo;variables&rdquo; to separate the first and second items in the series. Choice D is incorrect because a semicolon can&rsquo;t be used in this way to separate items in a simple series. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-663-SEC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"e8513e3e-e344-4549-a27c-848c952e8acc","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959345,"updateDate":1691007959345}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959345, 1691007959345),
    ($SATQ$e2759b92$SATQ$, $SATQ$6003973a-54bc-40dc-b4eb-46666a6f82ab$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$be08d87b-5e7a-492b-818f-186d4fb2ba6b$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$FSS$SATQ$, $SATQ$Form, Structure, and Sense$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Occupying a significant part of modern-day Nigeria, the Kingdom of Benin was one of the major powers in West Africa between the thirteenth and nineteenth centuries. It <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> ruled by Oba Ewuare I from 1440 to 1473.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"719a1bac-2cbc-4928-bf12-044fc3d4d8e1","content":"<p>is</p>"},{"id":"e5e756b4-a140-49d1-be9e-8374150d1db1","content":"<p>will be</p>"},{"id":"0c0ac160-8d70-4a3f-aba9-a933abdd8ee6","content":"<p>has been</p>"},{"id":"a2f40ece-5dfd-471b-b958-29ee2010c0ad","content":"<p>was</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["a2f40ece-5dfd-471b-b958-29ee2010c0ad"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of verbs to express tense in a sentence. In this choice, the past tense verb &ldquo;was ruled&rdquo; correctly indicates that Oba Ewuare I ruled the Kingdom of Benin in the distant past (from 1440 to 1473). This past tense verb choice is consistent with the other past tense verb (&ldquo;was&rdquo;) used to describe the Kingdom of Benin. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the present tense verb &ldquo;is ruled&rdquo; doesn&rsquo;t indicate that Oba Ewuare I ruled the Kingdom of Benin in the distant past. Choice B is incorrect because the future tense verb &ldquo;will be ruled&rdquo; doesn&rsquo;t indicate that Oba Ewuare I ruled the Kingdom of Benin in the distant past. Choice C is incorrect because the present perfect tense verb &ldquo;has been ruled&rdquo; doesn&rsquo;t indicate that Oba Ewuare I ruled the Kingdom of Benin in the distant past. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-FSS-E008$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$7815d2f7-78a7-462f-8260-87b40df72019$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"e2759b92","external_id":"6003973a-54bc-40dc-b4eb-46666a6f82ab","vaultid":"be08d87b-5e7a-492b-818f-186d4fb2ba6b","uId":"a5172cbd-fd52-418a-8a60-9905d0890ce1","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"FSS","skill_desc":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Occupying a significant part of modern-day Nigeria, the Kingdom of Benin was one of the major powers in West Africa between the thirteenth and nineteenth centuries. It <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> ruled by Oba Ewuare I from 1440 to 1473.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"719a1bac-2cbc-4928-bf12-044fc3d4d8e1","content":"<p>is</p>"},{"id":"e5e756b4-a140-49d1-be9e-8374150d1db1","content":"<p>will be</p>"},{"id":"0c0ac160-8d70-4a3f-aba9-a933abdd8ee6","content":"<p>has been</p>"},{"id":"a2f40ece-5dfd-471b-b958-29ee2010c0ad","content":"<p>was</p>"}],"keys":["a2f40ece-5dfd-471b-b958-29ee2010c0ad"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of verbs to express tense in a sentence. In this choice, the past tense verb &ldquo;was ruled&rdquo; correctly indicates that Oba Ewuare I ruled the Kingdom of Benin in the distant past (from 1440 to 1473). This past tense verb choice is consistent with the other past tense verb (&ldquo;was&rdquo;) used to describe the Kingdom of Benin. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the present tense verb &ldquo;is ruled&rdquo; doesn&rsquo;t indicate that Oba Ewuare I ruled the Kingdom of Benin in the distant past. Choice B is incorrect because the future tense verb &ldquo;will be ruled&rdquo; doesn&rsquo;t indicate that Oba Ewuare I ruled the Kingdom of Benin in the distant past. Choice C is incorrect because the present perfect tense verb &ldquo;has been ruled&rdquo; doesn&rsquo;t indicate that Oba Ewuare I ruled the Kingdom of Benin in the distant past. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-FSS-E008","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"7815d2f7-78a7-462f-8260-87b40df72019","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959366,"updateDate":1691007959366}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959366, 1691007959366),
